/// \defgroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories Stances, wall hubs, terrain income and other modules, and their factories
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Eight BFME 2 module classes (StancesBehavior, WallHubBehavior,
///        TerrainResourceBehavior, WargBehavior, PillageModule,
///        ProductionSpeedBonus, FellBeastSwoopPower and the data of
///        SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior), the instance factories that
///        create their modules and the data factories that parse their INI.
///
/// Every class here is a module that an \c Object INI block lists by name
/// on a module line. The group covers what each class is for, what its
/// module data reads from INI, and the two factory functions the module
/// factory reaches it through. Each class page summarises the bodies
/// examined for this group; other behaviour of these classes is not
/// described here. For what has been recovered, see the ledger
/// (`reverse/functions.csv`) and `python3 tools/progress.py`
/// (\ref page_mainpage_state).
///
/// \par The factory flow
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance
/// factory, a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the
/// registrations are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`.
/// The data factory runs while INI loads (ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI),
/// the instance factory when an object is built (ModuleFactory::newModule).
/// Neither is called directly. \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// describes the flow step by step and
/// \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories the shared code shape; both
/// apply here unchanged.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// - Every module name in this group is spelled like its class and is
///   registered as a logic (behavior) module, module type 0.
/// - Each registration has its own interface mask. Read with Zero Hour's
///   bit values (`Common/Module.h`; the BFME 2 meaning of the bits is not
///   confirmed here):
///   - StancesBehavior, WallHubBehavior and FellBeastSwoopPower: update.
///   - TerrainResourceBehavior: update, die and create.
///   - SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior: update and damage, the same pair
///     Zero Hour's class reports from its own \c getInterfaceMask.
///   - ProductionSpeedBonus: special power only, the same mask as
///     SpecialPowerModule and many other special-power registrations (for
///     example OCLSpecialPower and CashHackSpecialPower). Not every
///     special-power registration uses this mask; some register with other
///     masks.
///   - WargBehavior: zero, none of the bits.
///   - PillageModule: a single bit above the highest one Zero Hour defines,
///     so it has no Zero Hour name. No other registration in the list uses
///     it.
/// - The instance factories documented here belong to all eight classes
///   except SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior, whose instance factory lives
///   in another unit. The data factories documented here are those of
///   StancesBehavior, SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior,
///   TerrainResourceBehavior and WallHubBehavior. The other four
///   registrations point at data factories that are not documented in this
///   group: PillageModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///   ProductionSpeedBonusModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///   FellBeastSwoopPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData, and WargBehavior's
///   data factory.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory has the same frame: allocate the class's size
///   with the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory), run the class's
///   constructor with the thing and the module data inside an exception
///   frame that frees the block if the constructor throws, and return the
///   result as a Module pointer. When the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned. The FellBeastSwoopPower
///   factory is a few bytes longer than the others only because its class
///   is large enough that the size no longer fits the short immediate
///   encoding.
/// - Every data factory has one frame as well: allocate, run the data
///   class's default constructor inside an exception frame, then, when the
///   INI pointer is not null, call INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the new
///   data and the class's static \c buildFieldParse. Parsing happens after
///   the exception frame has closed, so an INI error thrown while parsing
///   does not free the new data, and the parse step does not test the
///   allocation for null.
/// - The four \c buildFieldParse functions behind these data factories each
///   add one table of the class's own fields and chain no base table.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from the
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has only SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior. Open-BFME-1's
/// \c game/ tree has code for WargBehavior, SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior,
/// ProductionSpeedBonus and FellBeastSwoopPower, and its module factory
/// registers them, so those classes already existed in BFME 1. It has no
/// code for StancesBehavior, WallHubBehavior, TerrainResourceBehavior or
/// PillageModule.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags and
///   the fields listed on the class pages. The field names there are read
///   from the retail field tables, including their retail spelling
///   (\c SpeedMulitplier, \c SelfHealSupression), and INI must use that
///   spelling.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, each one's module type and interface
///   mask, the constructor defaults quoted on the class pages and what each
///   class does with its fields. A new kind of module needs a new class and
///   a new ModuleFactory::init registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: these are logic modules and their data comes from
///   \c Object blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Fields read by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt (\c IncomeInterval,
/// \c SelfHealSupression and \c SelfHealDelay) are written in milliseconds
/// and turned into whole logic frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref page_frame_loop_modding). Fields read as plain integers are not
/// converted: ProductionSpeedBonus's \c NumberOfFrames, as its name says,
/// is a count of frames. A change to the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop)
/// therefore affects the two kinds of field differently.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow,
///      \ref grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories


/// \class StancesBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "StancesBehavior": holds its
///        object's current stance and applies the effects a StanceTemplate
///        definition gives each stance.
///
/// The module data names one \c StanceTemplate definition. StanceTemplate
/// is a top-level INI block of its own, and its definitions are kept in the
/// \c TheStancesStore subsystem (\ref page_startup lists it among the
/// subsystems the engine creates).
///
/// Summarised from the matched bodies:
/// - StancesBehavior::changeStance switches to a new stance. It looks the
///   module's StanceTemplate up in \c TheStancesStore; when the old and new
///   stances name different attribute modifiers it removes the old one from
///   the object's attribute-modifier pool and adds the new one
///   (Object::removeAttributeModifierFromPool,
///   Object::addAttributeModifierToPool). It passes a per-stance value to
///   the object's rider when there is one, and adjusts the object's AI for
///   some stances. When the stance's class changes, and when the first
///   stance is applied, it notifies the module's registered listeners. It
///   returns false when the stance does not change or no StanceTemplate is
///   found.
/// - Listeners hear about stance changes and about the module's
///   destruction: the destructor broadcasts to them before freeing the
///   list. The stance toggle command button of the in-game UI
///   (InGameToggleStanceCommandButton) is one such listener.
/// - StancesBehavior::update stays awake while its object has one
///   particular status; once that status is gone it applies the initial
///   stance if no stance has been set yet and then sleeps for good.
/// - StancesBehavior::xfer saves version 1, the update-module base and the
///   current stance.
///
/// The numeric stance values, the status the update waits for, the rider
/// call and the AI adjustments are not identified here. The class is not in
/// Zero Hour, and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has no code for it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: the \c StanceTemplate field picks a StanceTemplate
///   definition, and that definition supplies, per stance, the name of the
///   attribute modifier the stance applies. Attribute modifiers are looked
///   up by name, so a StanceTemplate entry and an attribute-modifier
///   definition must agree on spelling.
/// - Multiplayer: stance changes run in the game logic and change the
///   object's attribute modifiers, so they are part of the lockstep state.
/// - Saves: the stance is saved with the module (version 1).
///
/// \see StancesBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      StancesBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class StancesBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for StancesBehavior: the name key of one
///        StanceTemplate definition.
///
/// Its field table has a single entry, \c StanceTemplate, read by
/// NameKeyGenerator::parseStringAsNameKeyType, so the data stores the
/// definition's name key, not a pointer: the definition is looked up in
/// \c TheStancesStore each time the stance changes. The constructor leaves
/// the key at zero; without a \c StanceTemplate line (or with a name the
/// store does not hold) StancesBehavior::changeStance fails before it stores
/// the new stance, so the module never leaves its unset stance and applies
/// no modifiers.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// StancesBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.
///
/// \see StancesBehavior

/// \fn Module *StancesBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "StancesBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's StancesBehaviorModuleData, shared with
///                    every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see StancesBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *StancesBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "StancesBehavior": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail keeps one copy of the default constructor for data classes of
/// this size and shape; this factory builds its data with the copy that
/// LockWeaponCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData also uses, then parses
/// the \c StanceTemplate table (\ref grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// describes the shared constructor).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see StancesBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class WallHubBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "WallHubBehavior": an update module
///        configured by the templates of wall segments and caps, build
///        distances and option flags.
///
/// The field names suggest a wall hub: an object from which wall segments
/// (\c SegmentTemplateName, \c ElevatedSegmentTemplateName) and end caps
/// for hubs, cliffs, shores and borders are built out to
/// \c MaxBuildoutDistance. How the wall-building logic uses each field is
/// not described here.
///
/// What the constructor does (matched body):
/// - it asks for an update on the next frame;
/// - it hands the module's wall-hub interface and the \c Options flags to
///   a routine of its object, read as registering the interface with the
///   object;
/// - it copies \c BuilderRadius into the module, but when the field still
///   holds the data constructor's sentinel default, 9.876, it takes a value
///   from the object instead. Which object value that is, is not identified
///   here.
///
/// WallHubBehavior::xfer saves version 1 and the update-module base and
/// nothing of its own. The class is not in Zero Hour, and Open-BFME-1's
/// \c game/ tree has no code for it. WallHubBehavior is among the first
/// names ModuleFactory::init registers.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Leaving \c BuilderRadius out, or writing exactly 9.876, both mean "use
/// the object's own value"; the sentinel is a hard-coded constant.
///
/// \see WallHubBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      WallHubBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class WallHubBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for WallHubBehavior: segment and cap template names,
///        build distances and option flags.
///
/// Fields, from the retail table, in table order:
/// - \c SegmentTemplateName: a list of names, read by
///   INI::parseAsciiStringVectorAppend, so each further line appends to the
///   list instead of replacing it.
/// - \c DefaultSegmentTemplateName, \c HubCapTemplateName,
///   \c CliffCapTemplateName, \c ShoreCapTemplateName,
///   \c BorderCapTemplateName and \c ElevatedSegmentTemplateName: one name
///   each (INI::parseAsciiString).
/// - \c StaggeredBuildFactor: an integer (INI::parseInt), default 5.
/// - \c BuilderRadius: a real (INI::parseReal), default 9.876, the sentinel
///   the module replaces with an object value (see WallHubBehavior).
/// - \c MaxBuildoutDistance: a real, default 54321.0. How the wall logic
///   uses it is not established here.
/// - \c Options: a set of flag names read by INI::parseBitString32, default
///   none. The name list is the one that begins \c NEED_TARGET_ENEMY_OBJECT,
///   \c NEED_TARGET_NEUTRAL_OBJECT, \c NEED_TARGET_ALLY_OBJECT; Zero Hour
///   uses those names for command-button options (\c TheCommandOptionNames).
///   Which flags matter to a wall hub is not established.
///
/// The names are stored as strings, not resolved templates, and the data
/// destructor releases them. Built once per module line by
/// WallHubBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.
///
/// \see WallHubBehavior

/// \fn Module *WallHubBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "WallHubBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame; the constructor it runs hands the
/// module's wall-hub interface to the object (see WallHubBehavior).
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's WallHubBehaviorModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see WallHubBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *WallHubBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "WallHubBehavior": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// The group page describes the shared frame; the constructor defaults are
/// listed on the WallHubBehaviorModuleData page.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see WallHubBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class TerrainResourceBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "TerrainResourceBehavior": makes
///        its object a source of periodic income for its controlling player.
///
/// Summarised from the matched TerrainResourceBehavior::update:
/// - On its first update the module registers its object with a manager
///   owned by the game logic, passing \c Radius, \c Visible and
///   \c HighPriority, and marks itself registered. A newly built module
///   then sleeps until something wakes it.
/// - On later updates, when the object has a controlling player with the
///   player data the module needs, it computes an amount from
///   \c MaxIncome, a scale the module holds, a value read from the object
///   and \c UpgradeBonusPercent. The bonus applies only when \c Upgrade is
///   set and two player checks, one on \c Upgrade and one on
///   \c UpgradeMustBePresent, both pass; otherwise the factor is 1. The
///   product is rounded up to a whole number.
/// - When a filter held in the player's data accepts this object, the
///   amount is further scaled by a percentage from a table in the same
///   player data, indexed by a count of the player's objects. The count
///   includes only the player's objects that the filter accepts and that do
///   not have one particular status. When the count reaches the table's
///   size, the last entry applies; each object beyond that lowers it by two
///   percentage points, down to zero. An amount that was positive before
///   this scaling never drops below 1 through it.
/// - When the amount is positive it is added to the player's money and
///   shown above the object as floating text formatted with the
///   \c GUI:AddCash string in the player's colour. The amount is then
///   passed to the object's experience tracker, when the object has one
///   and a check on the tracker passes.
/// - The update then sleeps for \c IncomeInterval logic frames.
///
/// What sets the module's scale and wakes it after registration, the
/// manager's role and the meaning of the per-player table are not
/// identified here.
///
/// TerrainResourceBehavior::xfer has two versions. Version 2 adds the
/// registered flag; loading a version 1 save derives that flag from the
/// other saved flag. TerrainResourceBehavior::loadPostProcess wakes the
/// module again when it was not registered, so registration happens after
/// a load as well.
///
/// The client also reads this module's data: building placement looks up a
/// template's TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData, and the placement
/// feedback class PlaceTerrainResourceClaimantFeedback is constructed
/// with it. TerrainResourceClientBehavior
/// (\ref grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories) is a client module with
/// a related name; how the two work together is not established here. The
/// class is not in Zero Hour, and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has no code
/// for it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The floating text comes from the \c GUI:AddCash string-table entry
///   (\ref page_modding_strings), formatted with the amount.
/// - The two-point step per extra object and the floor of 1 on a payout
///   that was positive before the per-count scaling are hard-coded.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The payout is computed in floating point and rounded up to an integer
/// in the game logic, and the result goes straight into a player's money.
/// Every machine in a game must produce the same integer, so a remaster
/// that changes the compiler, the floating-point mode or the order of
/// these multiplications can desynchronise multiplayer games and replays.
///
/// \see TerrainResourceBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for TerrainResourceBehavior: registration radius,
///        income, interval and the upgrade bonus.
///
/// Fields, from the retail table, with the constructor defaults:
/// - \c Radius: a real (INI::parseReal), default 0. Passed to the manager
///   on registration.
/// - \c MaxIncome: an integer (INI::parseInt), default 0. The base of
///   every payout.
/// - \c IncomeInterval: a duration (INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt), stored
///   in logic frames; the update's sleep between payouts.
/// - \c HighPriority and \c Visible: booleans (INI::parseBool), defaults
///   false and true. Passed to the manager on registration.
/// - \c UpgradeMustBePresent: read by the object-filter parser
///   (iniParseObjectFilter) and used in the player check that gates the
///   bonus.
/// - \c Upgrade: an upgrade template (INI::parseUpgradeTemplate), default
///   none, which disables the bonus.
/// - \c UpgradeBonusPercent: a percentage (INI::parsePercentToReal),
///   default 100%, the income multiplier while the bonus applies.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.
///
/// \see TerrainResourceBehavior

/// \fn Module *TerrainResourceBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "TerrainResourceBehavior":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame; the constructor it runs asks for an
/// update on the next frame, which is when the module registers.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *TerrainResourceBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "TerrainResourceBehavior": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// The group page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see TerrainResourceBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior: when and how
///        fast a damaged object heals itself.
///
/// Fields, from the retail table: \c SelfHealSupression and
/// \c SelfHealDelay, durations read by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt and
/// stored in logic frames, and \c SelfHealAmount, a real (INI::parseReal).
/// The constructor sets all three to zero. These are Zero Hour's three
/// fields with Zero Hour's spelling, misspelt \c Supression included.
///
/// Zero Hour: the behavior suppresses self-healing for
/// \c SelfHealSupression after each hit, then heals \c SelfHealAmount every
/// \c SelfHealDelay until the object is at full health, and marks the
/// object's dock crippled while its body is really damaged. The BFME 2
/// module's own logic is not described here.
///
/// Zero Hour's \c buildFieldParse first runs UpdateModuleData's, which adds
/// no fields there, and then adds this table; the BFME 2 one adds only this
/// table. BFME 1 also has the class.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and
/// shared by every object of the template.
///
/// \see SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn ModuleData *SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// The group page describes the shared frame. Zero Hour generates the same
/// function from its module macro.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see SupplyWarehouseCripplingBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class WargBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "WargBehavior" with an empty
///        interface mask.
///
/// Its constructor builds directly on BehaviorModule and adds nothing but
/// its own virtual tables, and its registration sets none of the interface
/// bits, so the module factory records no update, damage, die or other
/// interface for it. BFME 1 registers it the same way, with an empty mask,
/// and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has code for the class, including an
/// update function; what the BFME 2 class does is not established here.
/// Its data factory is not documented in this group.
///
/// \see WargBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *WargBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "WargBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see WargBehavior


/// \class PillageModule
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Logic module registered as "PillageModule", configured by a
///        pillage amount, a number of damage events and an object filter.
///
/// Its data class, PillageModuleData, reads \c PillageAmount and
/// \c NumDamageEventsPerPillage as unsigned integers
/// (INI::parseUnsignedInt) and \c PillageFilter with the object-filter
/// parser (iniParseObjectFilter). The names suggest that the module pays
/// its owner \c PillageAmount after every \c NumDamageEventsPerPillage
/// damage events against objects that pass \c PillageFilter; this page does
/// not confirm that.
///
/// The registration's interface mask is a bit Zero Hour does not define,
/// and no other module in the registration list uses it. This suggests an
/// interface specific to BFME 2, but what the bit means in BFME 2 is not
/// established here. PillageModule::xfer saves
/// version 1, its base's state and one unsigned integer of its own. The
/// class is not in Zero Hour, and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has no code
/// for it.
///
/// \see PillageModule::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      PillageModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *PillageModule::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "PillageModule": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's PillageModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see PillageModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class ProductionSpeedBonus
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Special-power module registered as "ProductionSpeedBonus": when
///        used, it gives its controlling player a timed production-speed
///        bonus for the listed types.
///
/// Its data class, ProductionSpeedBonusModuleData, first takes the fields
/// of SpecialPowerModuleData (its \c buildFieldParse runs
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse) and adds three of its own:
/// \c NumberOfFrames (INI::parseInt), \c SpeedMulitplier (INI::parseReal,
/// retail spelling) and \c Type, a list of names (INI::parseAsciiStringVector).
///
/// ProductionSpeedBonus::doSpecialPower, the untargeted use of the power
/// (matched body):
/// - For each name in \c Type it records with the object's controlling
///   player a bonus keyed by that name: the value 1 / \c SpeedMulitplier
///   minus 1 (computed as (m - 1) / -m) and \c NumberOfFrames as its
///   duration. A multiplier of 2 thus stores -0.5, which fits a build time
///   divided by the multiplier; how production reads the value is not
///   described here, nor what kind of name \c Type holds.
/// - Then, unless one of the object's disabled flags is set, it calls one
///   more routine through its special-power interface with the object's
///   position and the same command options; the BFME 2 routine is not
///   identified here.
///
/// BFME 1's version of the body has the same formula and calls
/// doSpecialPowerAtLocation at the point of that last call.
///
/// The registration's mask is the special-power bit (Zero Hour's value),
/// the same as in BFME 1, which has the class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// \c NumberOfFrames is not converted from milliseconds: write logic
/// frames. \c SpeedMulitplier must be spelt as retail spells it and must
/// not be 0, since the bonus divides by it.
///
/// \see ProductionSpeedBonus::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories

/// \fn Module *ProductionSpeedBonus::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ProductionSpeedBonus": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ProductionSpeedBonusModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ProductionSpeedBonus


/// \class FellBeastSwoopPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_behavior_misc_module_factories
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as
///        "FellBeastSwoopPower": runs a swoop ability and tracks how close
///        its object flies to the ground.
///
/// The class belongs to the SpecialAbilityUpdate family
/// (\ref grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories): its data's
/// \c buildFieldParse first runs SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse
/// and then adds \c SpecialWeapon (INI::parseWeaponTemplate) and
/// \c WhichSpecialWeapon (INI::parseInt), and its save function extends
/// SpecialAbilityUpdate::xfer. Its update-only mask matches most other
/// special-ability update registrations.
///
/// Summarised from the matched bodies:
/// - FellBeastSwoopPower::triggerAbilityEffect, when the object has an AI,
///   issues an AI command from the AI command source at the ability's
///   target object if it still exists, otherwise at the ability's target
///   position. Which commands these are is not established here.
/// - On its first run, FellBeastSwoopPower::update calls triggerAbilityEffect
///   through the virtual table, which for this class is
///   FellBeastSwoopPower::triggerAbilityEffect, so the AI command described
///   above is issued; it then records that it has started. After that, when
///   the object's AI reports either of two conditions, it ends the ability
///   through FellBeastSwoopPower::onExit and sleeps for good; otherwise it
///   sets one of the object's model-condition flags while the object's
///   height above the terrain is below a dimension of its geometry, clears
///   it above, and asks to run again on the next frame.
/// - FellBeastSwoopPower::onExit clears that flag and the started marker.
/// - FellBeastSwoopPower::xfer saves version 1, the SpecialAbilityUpdate
///   state and the started marker.
///
/// The two AI conditions and the model condition are not identified here.
/// The class is not in Zero Hour; BFME 1 has it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// \c SpecialWeapon names a Weapon definition and \c WhichSpecialWeapon is
/// an integer; the bodies summarised above do not read them, so how they
/// are used is not described here.
///
/// \see FellBeastSwoopPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      FellBeastSwoopPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *FellBeastSwoopPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "FellBeastSwoopPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame; the constructor it runs clears the
/// started marker that FellBeastSwoopPower::update tests.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's FellBeastSwoopPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see FellBeastSwoopPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData
